.TH INITTAB 4 "31 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
inittab \- what init starts, at boot and at each run level
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/inittab
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR init (1M)
reads
.B /etc/inittab
at boot and whenever the run level changes, and starts, waits for or
restarts the commands it lists.
The file shipped with the base package is the system's own, from release
2.1p2a, with one line added.
.SS Format
Each entry is one line of four fields separated by colons:
.PP
.RS
.IB id : rstate : action : process
.RE
.TP
.I id
one to four characters naming the entry, unique in the file.
.TP
.I rstate
the run levels
.RB ( 0 \- 6 ,
.BR s )
at which the entry applies, written together (as in
.BR 23 );
empty for every level.
.TP
.I action
how
.B init
treats the process:
.RS
.TP 12
.B sysinit
run, and waited for, at boot before
.B init
asks for or enters a run level; these entries run in the order they are
written.
.TP
.B bootwait
run once, when the system first enters a listed run level, and waited for.
.TP
.B wait
run, and waited for, on entering a listed run level.
.TP
.B respawn
kept running: started again whenever it ends.
.TP
.B powerfail
run when
.B init
is told of a power failure.
.TP
.B initdefault
not a process: its
.I rstate
is the run level to enter at boot.
.RE
.TP
.I process
the command, given to
.BR sh (1)
to run; the rest of the line after a
.B #
is a comment.
.PP
A line that starts with
.B #
is a comment.
.SS The shipped entries
.PP
The first line sets run level 2 as the one the system boots to.
The
.B sysinit
entries then run, in this order:
.IP \(bu 3
.BR "dlm" ,
the line the base package adds:
.B /sbin/modadmin -B
loads the kernel modules marked for boot.
It is the first
.B sysinit
entry because the drivers it loads (the network, the console and the
monitor switch among them) must be there before the entries after it use
them.
.IP \(bu 3
.BR autopush (1M)
with
.BR /etc/amiga.ap ,
the STREAMS modules pushed on the console devices;
.IP \(bu 3
the console set-up
.RB ( sioc ,
and
.B /dev/syscon
linked to the console);
.IP \(bu 3
.BR /etc/sysinit ,
the system's own early checks.
.PP
After them come
.B /etc/brc
(at boot), one
.B /etc/rc
script for each run level
.RB ( rc0 ,
.BR rc1 ,
.BR rc2 ,
.BR rc3 ,
and
.B rc6
for a reboot), the power-failure shutdown, the service access
controller
.BR sac (1M),
and the console login.
Logins on the serial ports and the other screens are set up with
.BR pmadm (1M),
not here.
.PP
An update of the base package keeps an edited
.B /etc/inittab
and puts the new default beside it as
.BR /etc/inittab.new .
.PP
The system's own page for this file, where it is installed, is shown by
.BR "man -a inittab" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/inittab
this file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR init (1M),
.BR sh (1)
